## What 'scope' means

Scope determines where in your code a variable can be seen and used. JavaScript has three main kinds relevant to everyday code: **global scope**, **function scope**, and **block scope**.

A variable declared outside any function or block lives in **global scope** and can be accessed from anywhere in the program. This is convenient but risky in large programs, since any part of the code could accidentally overwrite it.

```js
let appName = "CodeAtlas"; // global scope

function printName() {
  console.log(appName); // visible here too
}
printName();
```

A variable declared with `let` or `const` inside a block (`{ }`, such as an `if` or `for`) is only visible within that block — this is **block scope**.

```js
if (true) {
  let secret = 42;
  console.log(secret); // 42, visible here
}
console.log(typeof secret); // "undefined" — not visible out here
```

A variable declared inside a function (with any keyword) is only visible inside that function — **function scope**. Each call to the function gets its own fresh set of local variables, independent of other calls.

```js
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0; // local to this function call
  count++;
  return count;
}
console.log(makeCounter()); // 1
console.log(makeCounter()); // 1 again — a brand new count each call
```

codeExamples.code:
if (true) {
  let secret = 42;
  console.log(secret);
}
console.log(typeof secret);

function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  count++;
  return count;
}
console.log(makeCounter());
console.log(makeCounter());

