interviewQuestions.answer:
Both are ordered collections, but they remove elements in opposite orders:

- A **stack** follows LIFO (Last In, First Out): the most recently added element is removed first. Think of a stack of books — you take the top one off first.
- A **queue** follows FIFO (First In, First Out): the earliest added element is removed first. Think of a line at a store — the first person in line is served first.

```js
// Stack (LIFO) using push/pop
let stack = [];
stack.push(1);
stack.push(2);
console.log(stack.pop()); // 2 — last one in, first one out

// Queue (FIFO) using push/shift
let queue = [];
queue.push(1);
queue.push(2);
console.log(queue.shift()); // 1 — first one in, first one out
```

Both can be implemented with a plain array; the only difference is which end you remove from.

One of the most important built-in uses of a stack is the **call stack**, which every programming language runtime uses to keep track of function calls. Each time a function is called, a new 'frame' (holding its local variables and where to resume afterward) is pushed onto the call stack; when the function returns, its frame is popped off, and execution resumes exactly where the calling function left off.

```js
function a() {
  b(); // calling b pushes a new frame onto the call stack
}
function b() {
  c(); // calling c pushes another frame
}
function c() {
  console.log("deepest point"); // call stack here: a -> b -> c
}
a();
```

When `c()` finishes, its frame pops off and control returns to `b()`; when `b()` finishes, control returns to `a()`. This LIFO behavior is exactly why recursive functions that never 'bottom out' cause a **'Maximum call stack size exceeded'** error — each recursive call pushes another frame, and without a stopping point, the stack keeps growing until it runs out of space.

Other practical examples include parsing matched brackets or parentheses in code (pushing an open bracket, popping when its matching close bracket appears), and the back-button history in a web browser.

codeExamples.code:
function factorial(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

console.log(factorial(5));
console.log(factorial(1));

